<section xml:id="sec-venn-diagrams" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Venn Diagrams</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.1 Sets
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Draw Venn diagrams to represent sets
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Drawing Venn diagrams for two and three sets and reading regions from them.
         Suggested activity: Groups survey 20 classmates on two or three subjects they like (e.g. Maths, Kiswahili, Agriculture) and draw a three-set Venn diagram of the results.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Three-set Venn diagram with input boxes for each region count; the totals n(A), n(B), n(C) and n(A∪B∪C) update automatically as learners type values. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLO: Core and Essential 1.1 (d). Lessons 5.1 and 5.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (survey of 20 classmates, three subjects)
         JSXGraph: fig-three-set-venn-counts (assets/jsxgraph/three-set-venn-counts.js), as planning notes
         Core-only: three-set formula (p-three-set-formula-core, ex-three-set-formula-check)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/01-sets/05-venn-diagrams/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 5.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-two-set-venn-diagrams">
        <title>Venn Diagrams with Two Sets</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the parts of a Venn diagram, the four regions and their numbers, and <m>n(A \cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cap B)</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> place elements in a diagram, describe shaded regions, and count.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> subject choices in a class.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners place numbers in a two-set Venn diagram and discover the rule for counting a union.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Draw the blank diagram on the board. Ask: <q>Which numbers go in the overlap?</q> before any are placed.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-multiples-of-two-and-three">
            <title>Multiples of Two and Three</title>
            <statement>
                <p><m>\xi = \{1, 2, \ldots, 15\}</m>, <m>T</m> is the set of multiples of <m>2</m> in <m>\xi</m> and <m>H</m> is the set of multiples of <m>3</m> in <m>\xi</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-multiples-of-two-and-three">
                    <caption>A blank Venn diagram for the sets <m>T</m> and <m>H</m>.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>A rectangle for the universal set with two overlapping circles labelled T and H. No numbers are written in yet.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (-2.45,1.75) {$T$};
\draw[thick] (1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (2.45,1.75) {$H$};
% regions
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Copy the diagram and write each number from <m>1</m> to <m>15</m> in the region where it belongs.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                                <p>Both: <m>6, 12</m>. <m>T</m> only: <m>2, 4, 8, 10, 14</m>. <m>H</m> only: <m>3, 9, 15</m>. Outside both: <m>1, 5, 7, 11, 13</m>.</p>
                                <figure xml:id="fig-multiples-of-two-and-three-filled">
                                    <caption>The numbers <m>1</m> to <m>15</m> placed in the diagram.</caption>
                                    <image width="60%">
                                        <shortdescription>The Venn diagram for T and H with the numbers placed. T only: 2, 4, 8, 10, 14. Both: 6, 12. H only: 3, 9, 15. Outside both: 1, 5, 7, 11, 13.</shortdescription>
                                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (-2.45,1.75) {$T$};
\draw[thick] (1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (2.45,1.75) {$H$};
% regions
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-1.8,0) {$2, 4, 8,$\\ $10, 14$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,0) {$6,$\\ $12$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (1.8,0) {$3, 9,$\\ $15$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-2.7,-2.35) {$1, 5, 7, 11, 13$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                                        </latex-image>
                                    </image>
                                </figure>
                            </solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find <m>n(T)</m>, <m>n(H)</m>, <m>n(T \cap H)</m> and <m>n(T \cup H)</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>7</m>, <m>5</m>, <m>2</m> and <m>10</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find a rule that links the four numbers in part (b).</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>10 = 7 + 5 - 2</m>: adding <m>n(T)</m> and <m>n(H)</m> counts <m>6</m> and <m>12</m> twice, so subtract <m>n(T \cap H)</m> once.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name the regions of a two-set diagram and state the counting rule.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) is the rule <m>n(A \cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cap B)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Fill the overlap first, then the rest of each circle, then the outside.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A shaded set can be described by its region numbers.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-two-set-venn-diagrams">
            <title>Two-Set Venn Diagrams</title>
            <p>In a <term>Venn diagram</term> the universal set <m>\xi</m> is a rectangle and each set is a circle, labelled outside the circle. Elements, or counts of elements, are written in the regions. In this book the four regions of a two-set diagram are numbered as in <xref ref="fig-two-set-regions"/>: region <m>1</m> is in <m>A</m> only, region <m>2</m> is in both, region <m>3</m> is in <m>B</m> only and region <m>4</m> is outside both. So <m>A</m> is regions <m>\{1, 2\}</m>, <m>B</m> is <m>\{2, 3\}</m> and <m>A \cup B</m> is <m>\{1, 2, 3\}</m>.</p>
            <p>For counting, fill the region that belongs to the most sets first, then work outwards. Adding <m>n(A)</m> and <m>n(B)</m> counts region <m>2</m> twice, so
                <md>n(A \cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cap B)</md></p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-two-set-regions">
            <caption>The four regions of a two-set Venn diagram.</caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>A rectangle for the universal set with two overlapping circles A and B. Region 1 is A only, region 2 is both, region 3 is B only, region 4 is outside both.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (-2.45,1.75) {$A$};
\draw[thick] (1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (2.45,1.75) {$B$};
% regions
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (-1.8,0) {1};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (0,0) {2};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (1.8,0) {3};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (-2.7,-2.35) {4};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-complements-by-region">
            <title>Complements by Region</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Give the region numbers of <m>A'</m>, <m>B'</m>, <m>(A \cup B)'</m> and <m>A' \cap B'</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>A' = \{3, 4\}</m> and <m>B' = \{1, 4\}</m>. <m>A \cup B = \{1, 2, 3\}</m>, so <m>(A \cup B)' = \{4\}</m>. <m>A' \cap B' = \{3, 4\} \cap \{1, 4\} = \{4\}</m>. These are two ways to describe the outside of both circles.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-agriculture-and-computer-studies">
            <title>Agriculture and Computer Studies</title>
            <statement>
                <p>In a class of <m>45</m> learners, <m>28</m> take Agriculture, <m>22</m> take Computer Studies and <m>9</m> take both. How many take Agriculture only, Computer Studies only, and neither? Find <m>n(A \cup C)</m> by the formula.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Fill the overlap first: <m>9</m>. Agriculture only <m>= 28 - 9 = 19</m>; Computer Studies only <m>= 22 - 9 = 13</m>; neither <m>= 45 - (19 + 9 + 13) = 4</m>. By the formula, <m>n(A \cup C) = 28 + 22 - 9 = 41 = 45 - 4</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-agriculture-and-computer-studies">
                    <caption>Agriculture and Computer Studies in a class of <m>45</m>.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>A two-set Venn diagram, Agriculture and Computer Studies. Agriculture only 19, both 9, Computer Studies only 13, neither 4.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Region counts: Agriculture only <m>19</m>; both <m>9</m>; Computer Studies only <m>13</m>; neither <m>4</m>. Total <m>45</m>.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (-1.9,2.2) {Agriculture};
\draw[thick] (1,0) circle (1.7);
\node at (1.9,2.2) {Computer Studies};
% regions
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-1.8,0) {$19$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,0) {$9$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (1.8,0) {$13$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-2.7,-2.35) {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-finding-the-overlap">
            <title>Finding the Overlap</title>
            <statement>
                <p>In a youth group of <m>38</m>, <m>25</m> have a smartphone, <m>21</m> have a bicycle and <m>5</m> have neither. How many have both?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>n(S \cup B) = 38 - 5 = 33</m>. Then <m>n(S \cap B) = n(S) + n(B) - n(S \cup B) = 25 + 21 - 33 = 13</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example <c>{2, 5, 9}</c>. On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap <c>?123</c> (or <c>123</c>), then <c>=\&lt;</c> (or <c>#+=</c>). Type the empty set as <c>{}</c>. Type a count as a whole number.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-regions-of-a-two-set-venn-diagram">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-regions-of-a-two-set-venn-diagram" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/01-sets/05-venn-diagrams/Regions-of-a-two-set-Venn-diagram.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-two-set-venn-diagrams">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-multiples-of-three-venn">
                <statement><p><m>\xi = \{1, 2, \ldots, 20\}</m>, <m>A</m> is the set of multiples of <m>3</m> and <m>B</m> is the set of odd numbers in <m>\xi</m>. Draw the Venn diagram. List <m>A \cap B</m> and the elements outside both circles.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>A</m> only <m>\{6, 12, 18\}</m>; <m>A \cap B = \{3, 9, 15\}</m>; <m>B</m> only <m>\{1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19\}</m>; outside both <m>\{2, 4, 8, 10, 14, 16, 20\}</m>.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-region-numbers-two-sets">
                <statement><p>Using the region numbers of <xref ref="fig-two-set-regions"/>, give the regions of (a) <m>B'</m> (b) <m>A \cap (A \cap B)'</m> (c) <m>(B - A) \cup (A \cup B)'</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>\{1, 4\}</m> (b) <m>\{1\}</m> (c) <m>\{3, 4\}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sport-and-choir">
                <statement><p>Of <m>40</m> learners, <m>26</m> play a sport, <m>18</m> sing in the choir and <m>7</m> do neither. How many do both? How many play a sport only? How many sing in the choir only?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Both <m>11</m>; sport only <m>15</m>; choir only <m>7</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>n(S \cup C) = 40 - 7 = 33</m>, so both <m>= 26 + 18 - 33 = 11</m>. Sport only <m>= 26 - 11 = 15</m> and choir only <m>= 18 - 11 = 7</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-formula-union">
                <statement><p><m>n(A) = 34</m>, <m>n(B) = 27</m> and <m>n(A \cap B) = 12</m>. Find <m>n(A \cup B)</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>49</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-formula-reverse">
                <statement><p><m>n(A \cup B) = 52</m>, <m>n(A) = 30</m> and <m>n(A \cap B) = 9</m>. Find <m>n(B)</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>31</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-can-they-miss">
                <title>Challenge: Must They Meet?</title>
                <statement><p><m>n(\xi) = 30</m>, <m>n(A) = 20</m> and <m>n(B) = 15</m>. Can <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have no element in common?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>No. <m>20 + 15 = 35 \gt 30</m>, so at least <m>5</m> elements are in both.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-two-set-venn-diagrams">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Work through the activity, <xref ref="example-complements-by-region"/> and <xref ref="example-agriculture-and-computer-studies"/>. Give a card with <xref ref="fig-two-set-regions"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-multiples-of-three-venn"/> and <xref ref="ex-sport-and-choir"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-multiples-of-three-venn"/> to <xref ref="ex-formula-reverse"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-can-they-miss"/>.</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 5.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-set-venn-diagrams">
        <title>Venn Diagrams with Three Sets</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the eight regions of a three-set diagram and their numbers; the centre-out method.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Know (Core):</alert> the formula for <m>n(A \cup B \cup C)</m> as a check.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> place elements in three sets, fill counts from totals, and read counts from a diagram.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> a class survey of subjects; crops grown by farmers.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Groups survey <m>20</m> classmates on three subjects and draw the three-set diagram; then they check their totals with the interactive diagram.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Each group asks <m>20</m> classmates which of Mathematics, Kiswahili and Agriculture they like. If time is short, use the table below. Remind learners to ask politely and to keep classmates' answers private.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-three-subjects">
            <title>Three Subjects</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Twenty learners said which of Mathematics (<m>M</m>), Kiswahili (<m>K</m>) and Agriculture (<m>Ag</m>) they like.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-three-subjects-survey">
                    <title>Subjects Liked by Twenty Learners</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell halign="left">Learner</cell><cell>Mathematics</cell><cell>Kiswahili</cell><cell>Agriculture</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Akinyi</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Brian</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Chepkoech</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>David</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Esther</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Fatuma</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>George</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Halima</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Ibrahim</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Jeptoo</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Kamau</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Lilian</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Mwangi</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Nafula</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Otieno</cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Pendo</cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Rehema</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Samson</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Tabitha</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Wekesa</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Draw a three-set Venn diagram and write the number of learners in each region.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>With the region numbers of <xref ref="fig-three-set-regions"/> (<m>M</m>, <m>K</m>, <m>Ag</m> as <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>): regions <m>1</m> to <m>8</m> hold <m>4, 1, 3, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find <m>n(M)</m>, <m>n(K)</m> and <m>n(Ag)</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>9</m>, <m>8</m> and <m>9</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How many learners like exactly two of the subjects? How many like at least one?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Exactly two: <m>1 + 2 + 2 = 5</m>. At least one: <m>20 - 3 = 17</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Enter your own class's counts in <xref ref="fig-three-set-venn-counts"/> and check your totals.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Answers depend on the class. With the table above the interactive shows <m>9</m>, <m>8</m>, <m>9</m>, <m>17</m> and <m>20</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name the eight regions and set out the centre-out method.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> each learner in the table is in exactly one of the eight regions.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A three-set diagram has eight regions. Fill the centre first and check every set total at the end.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-three-set-venn-diagrams">
            <title>Three-Set Venn Diagrams</title>
            <p>A three-set diagram has eight regions, numbered as in <xref ref="fig-three-set-regions"/>: <m>1</m> in <m>A</m> only, <m>2</m> in <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> only, <m>3</m> in <m>B</m> only, <m>4</m> in <m>A</m> and <m>C</m> only, <m>5</m> in all three, <m>6</m> in <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> only, <m>7</m> in <m>C</m> only, <m>8</m> outside all three.</p>
            <p>When a survey gives the set totals, the pair totals and the number in all three, fill the region that belongs to the most sets first, then work outwards:
                <ol>
                    <li><p>Write the number in all three in the centre.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Each <q>exactly two</q> region is its pair total minus the centre.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Each <q>only</q> region is its set total minus its two <q>exactly two</q> regions minus the centre.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The number in none is the total minus the seven inner regions.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Check every set total.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </insight>

        <p xml:id="p-three-set-formula-core" component="core">The counting rule for two sets extends to three sets, and gives a check on your diagram:
            <md>n(A \cup B \cup C) = n(A) + n(B) + n(C) - n(A \cap B) - n(A \cap C) - n(B \cap C) + n(A \cap B \cap C)</md></p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-three-set-regions">
            <caption>The eight regions of a three-set Venn diagram.</caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Three overlapping circles A, B and C in a rectangle, with the eight regions numbered 1 to 8: 1 A only, 2 A and B only, 3 B only, 4 A and C only, 5 all three, 6 B and C only, 7 C only, 8 outside all three.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-0.85,0.55) circle (1.55);
\node at (-2.35,2.1) {$A$};
\draw[thick] (0.85,0.55) circle (1.55);
\node at (2.35,2.1) {$B$};
\draw[thick] (0,-0.9) circle (1.55);
\node at (1.6,-2.4) {$C$};
% regions
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (-1.6,1.0) {1};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (0,1.35) {2};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (1.6,1.0) {3};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (-1.05,-0.55) {4};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (0,0.05) {5};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (1.05,-0.55) {6};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (0,-1.85) {7};
\node[circle,draw,inner sep=1.5pt,fill=white,font=\small] at (-3.4,-2.4) {8};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-three-set-venn-counts">
            <caption>A three-set Venn diagram that adds up its own totals. Type a count in each region.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-three-set-venn-counts" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/three-set-venn-counts.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-three-set-venn-counts" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Type a whole number in each of the eight <alert>region boxes</alert>. The diagram starts with the survey of <xref ref="act-three-subjects"/>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The totals <m>n(A)</m>, <m>n(B)</m>, <m>n(C)</m>, <m>n(A \cup B \cup C)</m> and the grand total update as you type. A warning appears if a box is not a whole number.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Try the crop survey of <xref ref="example-crops-in-bungoma"/>: you should see <m>35</m>, <m>29</m>, <m>24</m>, <m>58</m> and <m>65</m>.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-placing-numbers-in-three-sets">
            <title>Placing Numbers</title>
            <statement>
                <p><m>\xi = \{1, 2, \ldots, 15\}</m>, <m>A</m> is the set of odd numbers, <m>B</m> the set of prime numbers and <m>C</m> the set of factors of <m>70</m> in <m>\xi</m>. List the elements in each of the eight regions.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>A = \{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15\}</m>, <m>B = \{2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13\}</m>, <m>C = \{1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14\}</m>. Start with the centre.</p>
                <p>Region <m>5</m> (all three): <m>\{5, 7\}</m>. Region <m>2</m>: <m>\{3, 11, 13\}</m>. Region <m>4</m>: <m>\{1\}</m>. Region <m>6</m>: <m>\{2\}</m>. Region <m>1</m>: <m>\{9, 15\}</m>. Region <m>3</m>: <m>\varnothing</m>. Region <m>7</m>: <m>\{10, 14\}</m>. Region <m>8</m>: <m>\{4, 6, 8, 12\}</m>.</p>
                <p>Region <m>3</m> is empty: every prime in <m>\xi</m> is odd or a factor of <m>70</m>. An empty region is still drawn.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-crops-in-bungoma">
            <title>Crops in Bungoma</title>
            <statement>
                <p>An agricultural officer asked <m>65</m> farmers in Bungoma which of maize, beans and bananas they grow. <m>35</m> grow maize, <m>29</m> beans and <m>24</m> bananas; <m>15</m> grow maize and beans, <m>11</m> maize and bananas, and <m>10</m> beans and bananas; <m>6</m> grow all three. How many grow maize only? How many grow exactly two of the crops? How many grow none of them?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><p>Centre: <m>6</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Exactly two: maize and beans only <m>15 - 6 = 9</m>; maize and bananas only <m>11 - 6 = 5</m>; beans and bananas only <m>10 - 6 = 4</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Only one: maize <m>35 - 9 - 5 - 6 = 15</m>; beans <m>29 - 9 - 4 - 6 = 10</m>; bananas <m>24 - 5 - 4 - 6 = 9</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>None: the seven inner regions add up to <m>58</m>, so none <m>= 65 - 58 = 7</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Check: maize <m>15 + 9 + 5 + 6 = 35</m>, beans <m>9 + 10 + 4 + 6 = 29</m>, bananas <m>5 + 4 + 9 + 6 = 24</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p>So <m>15</m> grow maize only, <m>9 + 5 + 4 = 18</m> grow exactly two crops and <m>7</m> grow none.</p>
                <p component="core">Check with the formula, writing <m>M</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>N</m> for the farmers who grow maize, beans and bananas: <m>n(M \cup B \cup N) = 35 + 29 + 24 - 15 - 11 - 10 + 6 = 58</m>, and <m>65 - 58 = 7</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-crops-in-bungoma">
                    <caption>Crops grown by <m>65</m> farmers in Bungoma.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>A three-set Venn diagram, maize, beans and bananas, with the number of farmers in each region.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Maize only <m>15</m>; maize and beans only <m>9</m>; beans only <m>10</m>; maize and bananas only <m>5</m>; all three <m>6</m>; beans and bananas only <m>4</m>; bananas only <m>9</m>; none <m>7</m>.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
% universal set
\draw[thick] (-4,-2.8) rectangle (4,2.8);
\node at (-3.6,2.4) {$\xi$};
% sets (solid circles, labelled outside)
\draw[thick] (-0.85,0.55) circle (1.55);
\node at (-2.35,2.1) {Maize};
\draw[thick] (0.85,0.55) circle (1.55);
\node at (2.35,2.1) {Beans};
\draw[thick] (0,-0.9) circle (1.55);
\node at (1.6,-2.4) {Bananas};
% regions
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-1.6,1.0) {$15$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,1.35) {$9$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (1.6,1.0) {$10$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-1.05,-0.55) {$5$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,0.05) {$6$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (1.05,-0.55) {$4$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (0,-1.85) {$9$};
\node[font=\small,align=center] at (-3.4,-2.4) {$7$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-reading-the-diagram">
            <title>Reading the Diagram</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use <xref ref="fig-crops-in-bungoma"/>. How many farmers grow at least two of the crops? Exactly one? Beans but not bananas?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>At least two: <m>9 + 5 + 4 + 6 = 24</m>. Exactly one: <m>15 + 10 + 9 = 34</m>. Beans but not bananas: beans only and maize and beans only, <m>10 + 9 = 19</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example <c>{2, 5, 9}</c>. On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap <c>?123</c> (or <c>123</c>), then <c>=\&lt;</c> (or <c>#+=</c>). Type the empty set as <c>{}</c>. Type a count as a whole number.</p>

